### Resolution, 21 November 2026

The user authorised closure if the evidence was sufficient. The review supports retaining RoBERTa scores as a useful but imperfect measure of positivity or negativity in selected Guardian coverage. Signing dates anchor the before-and-after comparison and the same scoring method applies on both sides. The main corpus uses name-matching rules. Human review checked player identity and tone on 200 sampled sentences, with four wrong-person labels excluded from the validation comparison. Across the 196 remaining cases, RoBERTa had the highest observed rank agreement (Spearman ρ = 0.5158, 95% bootstrap interval 0.3839–0.6314). The 14 older-player cases leave age-specific validity uncertain. Passing mentions and quoted speech can contribute to the main scores. The focus rule was rejected as a main filter. Full article bodies, sentence inputs and score caches remain unavailable, which limits corpus reproducibility. [Validation results](../../data/dataset_nlp/results/b9_model_scores.csv) · [Evidence, wording and table](../evidence_and_sources.md#issue-04-guardian-tone-measurement-review-21-november-2026)

Use the adapted measurement and validation wording from the earlier draft as the basis for the new paper and retain its four-instrument table. The focus-rule details are in the evidence inventory. No further analysis or rerun is required for this qualified measurement claim. [Issue 07](2026-11-19_15-30-34_07-research-literature-and-verify-citations.scratch#sentiment-citation-to-consider) records the user's suggested RoBERTa-BiLSTM related-work citation. The [storyline](../paper_storyline.md#guardian-tone-measurement-21-november-2026), [checkpoint](../README.md#current-checkpoint), [tracker](README.md) and [repository context](REPOSITORY_CONTEXT.md) record the handoff. Next is the interactive [Issue 05](2026-11-19_15-30-34_05-verify-text-results-and-exploratory-status.scratch) review of H2, exploratory H2b and coverage volume, beginning with what each outcome measures.
